course: dictionary
lesson: 10676
title: Unofficial
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Look at the start of the word: un- means not, so unofficial means not official. Official means approved or agreed by the people in charge. An unofficial thing has not been approved by them.
explain: Read the words around it to check. «Do not download paid games for free from unofficial sites; they often carry viruses or steal information.» The words paid and free tell you the sites are not allowed to give those games away, and viruses tell you they may be unsafe.
explain: Unofficial does not always mean bad. A game you make up at break with your friends is unofficial, because your teacher did not set it. An unofficial result in sport is one the judges have not checked yet.
explain: To test your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Put in not approved instead of unofficial. If the sentence still makes sense, your meaning is right.
fact: Official means approved or agreed by the people in charge.
fact: The prefix un- means not, so unofficial means not official.
fact: «Unofficial» means not done or agreed by the people in charge.
fact: An unofficial result has not yet been checked by the judges.
fact: Malware is software that spies, steals or breaks things, and downloads from unofficial sites often carry it. Official app stores check apps, though not perfectly.
exercise: «Do not download paid games for free from unofficial sites.» What does unofficial mean here? | *Not approved by the people in charge | Very old and famous | Free and fun to play | Official, checked and approved by the makers
exercise: Ruby and her friends make up their own game at break. The teacher did not set it. Their game is an what game? | ancient | *unofficial | famous | official
exercise: Find and copy the word in «Free copies of paid games on unofficial sites often carry viruses that steal passwords.» that tells you the sites are not approved. | copies | passwords | viruses | *unofficial
test: A judge says the race result is unofficial. What does this mean? | It was won by the fastest runner ever | It was checked twice and is final | It was a race for grown-ups only | *It has not yet been checked by the judges
test: Which is more likely to carry malware? | *An unofficial site offering paid games for free | A game you make up with friends at break | An official app store, which checks its apps
exam: Which of these is an unofficial game? | A game your teacher set for the whole class | A game approved by the people in charge of the club | A game the school sports day judges checked | *A game you and your friends invented at break
